+---
+id: dl-004-applymergepatch-non-mutating-rfc-7386-semantics-with-null-deleting-members
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch: non-mutating RFC 7386 semantics with null deleting members"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+
+# Context
+
+Clients send partial updates as JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386). The library already has `applyPatch`
+(dl-002), which is non-mutating and treats `__proto__` as an ordinary key.
+
+# Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch` follows the RFC 7386 §2 algorithm: a `null` member deletes, other members merge
+  recursively, and a non-object patch (array, scalar, `null`) replaces the target, so `null` can be a result.
+- A non-object target merged with an object patch is treated as `{}`; `null` members inside are dropped.
+- Arrays are replaced, never merged, and `null` inside an array is kept as a value.
+- Neither argument is mutated and the result shares no structure with either (deep copies), as in `applyPatch`.
+- Members are set as own data properties, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key and cannot pollute prototypes.
+- It never throws: every JSON value is a valid patch.
+
+# Consequences
+
+Behaviour is the same as `applyPatch` for aliasing and safety. A merge patch cannot set a member to
+`null` or edit array elements; that is inherent to the format, and `applyPatch` covers those cases.
